// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma hyperion >=0.0.1;

import "./interfaces/IArkTimelock.hyp";
import "./interfaces/IArkStakeGovernable.hyp";
import "./interfaces/IObjectionSource.hyp";
import "./utils/ArkEpoch.hyp";
import "./utils/ArkGovTables.hyp";
import "./utils/QuantumErrors.hyp";

/// The accepting half of IGovernanceHandover (contracts/staking): what `acceptGovernanceOf` calls.
interface IGovernanceAccept {
    function acceptGovernance() external;
}

/**
 * ArkTimelock — `governance` of ArkStakeParams, ArkStakeRegistry and
 * ArkBondDelegation (design §6.1, §6.2, §7).
 *
 * WHY THIS ALONE CLOSES AUDIT D9
 * ------------------------------
 * The minimum delay is compiled in and looked up from the payload, so the S1
 * multisig — before any governor exists — already cannot move the threat stage
 * faster than one step per 30 days, cannot reprice the ladder around that rule
 * (D5), and cannot move any of the 39 value-transfer keys sooner than 30 days or
 * by more than a quarter of its range per operation (D7, EC-06). Every layer
 * holds on its own (design N2): the target still
 * checks ranges and relations; this contract checks pace.
 *
 * WHAT THE GUARDIAN CAN AND CANNOT DO (G14)
 * -----------------------------------------
 * Cancel a Queued operation. It cannot schedule, execute, pause, or touch
 * anything that does not pass through here — in particular not the Q-Day
 * ratification or the court's governance step, which never queue. The one
 * exception is outside this contract: contracts/staking's valuation oracle
 * reads `guardian()` and lets it pause the oracle directly, once, for
 * `pauseMaxEpochs`; only an operation of this timelock (`pause()`, seven
 * days, objectable at S1) renews it, and `renounceGuardian` ends it (yellow
 * paper §6.1).
 *
 * NO FUNDS
 * --------
 * There is no `receive`. A payable operation is funded by whoever executes it,
 * with exactly the value fixed at scheduling, so the timelock never holds QRL a
 * captured proposer could route.
 *
 * LEG B OBJECTION AT S1 (design §6.8, D6 from S1 by EC-06)
 * --------------------------------------------------------
 * At S1 the proposer is a multisig and no vote follows, so the chamber the
 * governor holds from S2 has nothing to act on. Here, an operation that writes
 * a value-transfer key or steps the threat stage, scheduled before the first
 * `handOver`, may be objected to by bond holders with the principal they held
 * at the boundary opening the epoch it was queued in. Reaching
 * `OBJECTION_THRESHOLD_BPS` of that epoch's total principal contests it: it is
 * not executed when its delay elapses, but waits a second delay of its keys
 * from the close of the objection, within which the guardian may cancel it. It
 * is escalation, not a veto, as at S2. The yellow paper §4.5 "contested
 * operation" row at S1: no quorum, no approval, twice the delay.
 *
 * The threshold is a constant at the design reference value (2,000 bps; the
 * governor's S2 chamber takes it as an immutable). The constructor is
 * unchanged, because contracts/staking redeploys this contract from its
 * vendored artifact with (proposer, guardian).
 *
 * THE HANDOVER IS AN OPERATION (re-audit 2026-09-30, SWAPGOV-03)
 * --------------------------------------------------------------
 * `handOver` ends the S1 chamber, so it runs only as a 30-day self-operation,
 * objectable at S1 like a value transfer, and only to the governor the
 * proposer named once (`setGovernor`). The S1 multisig used to call it
 * directly, to any account, and close the chamber at once.
 *
 * ONE QUEUED OPERATION PER KEY (re-audit 2026-09-30, SWAPGOV-04; GV-01)
 * --------------------------------------------------------------------
 * A keyed diff that names a key another Queued operation names is refused
 * (`KeyPending`): the step of each operation is measured against its own
 * `expected`, so concurrent operations chained their steps across a key's
 * whole range after one delay.
 *
 * A STAGE STEP IN THE QUEUE IS PUBLIC (QL-25, PV-20)
 * --------------------------------------------------
 * `stageStepQueued()` is true while an operation that steps the threat stage
 * (`setThreatStage`) of the caller is Queued: scheduled, not executed, not
 * cancelled — counted per target since the re-audit of 2026-09-30
 * (SWAPGOV-09), so a stage payload aimed at an account that is no parameter
 * set holds no commitment shut. The staking parameter set asks for itself;
 * the registry writes it into the allocation leaf, and the escrow refuses
 * a term commitment while it stands, because a commitment made inside the
 * thirty days would lock the rung the step is about to leave. It is a count
 * kept on schedule, execute and cancel, plus the latest time a counted
 * operation can still execute (eta + GRACE_PERIOD, moved by a contest). An
 * operation that expires unexecuted is not uncounted, so the view also reads
 * false once that latest time has passed, and the next stage step scheduled
 * after it starts the count afresh. The one residue errs toward refusing: an
 * operation that expired while a later one was queued stays counted until the
 * later one's latest time (since OC-GOV-02 a later one cannot be queued while
 * an earlier one is, so the residue no longer arises).
 *
 * AN ECOSYSTEM PHASE STEP IS A STAGE STEP FOR EVERY READER (2026-10-07)
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 * `setPhase`, `enterQDay` and `restore` (ArkPhase, `QuantumArk-Phases.md`
 * §3) move the phase every product reads, and through it Ark Staking's
 * threat stage. They are counted once, for the network rather than for their
 * target (there is one ArkPhase per network and the parameter set asking is
 * never that target), so `stageStepQueued()` and `stageStepQueuedFor(any)`
 * are true while one is Queued, and `phaseStepQueued()` reads that count
 * alone. A phase payload aimed at an account that is not ArkPhase is counted
 * as well: it holds commitments shut exactly as a real one would, which the
 * proposer could schedule anyway, and the guardian cancels either. A
 * `setPhase` or `restore` is refused (`StagePending`) while any phase
 * operation is Queued; `enterQDay` never is, so the 48-hour defensive step
 * cannot be held behind a 30-day one (ArkPhase itself refuses a second
 * transition in one epoch). All three are objectable at S1 (Phases doc R2:
 * Leg B and the guardian can stop a captured emergency step).
 *
 * ONE QUEUED STAGE STEP PER TARGET (OC round 2026-10-03, OC-GOV-02)
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------
 * A `setThreatStage` operation is refused (`StagePending`) while another for
 * the same target is Queued. Counted only, the S1 multisig queued 1->2, 2->3
 * and 3->4 at once, and with the target's one step per epoch stage 4 was in
 * force some thirty days and two epochs later, where the papers promise three
 * thirty-day delays in sequence.
 *
 * WHAT ENDS OR REDIRECTS A CHAMBER IS OBJECTABLE (OC-GOV-01)
 * ----------------------------------------------------------
 * Beside the stage and the value-transfer keys, an S1 operation that names
 * `proposeGovernance` (a governed contract's next governance accepts and then
 * answers to no timelock), `setObjectionSource` or `setCourt` (set once: a
 * false value ends the S2 chamber or the court's terminal step for good) is
 * objectable like the handover. `ruleFor` returns the flag beside the delay,
 * so the governor reads the same set instead of mirroring it.
 *
 * ACCEPTING GOVERNANCE AT ONCE (OC-GOV-03)
 * ----------------------------------------
 * `acceptGovernanceOf(target)` lets anyone have this contract accept the
 * governance a target has proposed to it, in the same transaction. Accepting
 * only moves power INTO the delays, so it needs none of its own; the deployer
 * of the staking leg proposes and has it accepted in one run, and no deployer
 * key governs past it (the 48-hour `acceptGovernance()` row stays for a
 * handover made the slow way).
 *
 * THE GUARDIAN'S LIMITS (GV-03, GV-17)
 * ------------------------------------
 * The guardian cannot cancel the operation that removes it (`renounceGuardian`)
 * nor an uncontested handover, and it cannot renounce itself: only the S3
 * operation removes it. The proposer and the guardian are distinct accounts.
 */
contract ArkTimelock is IArkTimelock {
    uint64 public constant GRACE_PERIOD = 14 days;

    /// Largest keyed diff one operation may carry; bounds the duplicate scan.
    uint256 public constant MAX_DIFF = 32;

    /// Leg B objection, as a share of bond principal, that contests an S1 operation (reference value).
    uint16 public constant OBJECTION_THRESHOLD_BPS = 2000;

    uint256 private constant BPS = 10000;

    /// The count key of ecosystem phase steps in `_stageSteps` / `_stageUntil`: no target is zero.
    address private constant PHASE_STEPS = address(0);

    struct Op {
        address target;
        uint64 eta;
        uint8 state;
        /// Writes a value-transfer key or steps the stage, and was scheduled at S1.
        bool objectable;
        bool contested;
        /// Steps the threat stage: counted by `stageStepQueued` while Queued.
        bool stageStep;
        /// Moves the ecosystem phase: counted under `PHASE_STEPS`, not its target.
        bool phaseStep;
        uint64 delay;
        uint64 queueEpoch;
        uint256 value;
        uint256 objection;
        bytes payload;
    }

    address public proposer;
    address public guardian;
    /// Leg B principal (IObjectionSource); set once.
    address public objectionSource;
    /// Set by the first `handOver`: from then on the governor's chamber covers what it queues.
    bool public handedOver;
    /// The one account `handOver` may name: contracts/core's ArkGovernor, set once (re-audit 2026-09-30, SWAPGOV-03).
    address public governor;

    mapping(bytes32 => Op) private _ops;
    mapping(bytes32 => mapping(address => bool)) private _objected;
    uint256 private _nonce;
    /// Per target: stage steps scheduled and neither executed nor cancelled (expired ones included) (SWAPGOV-09).
    mapping(address => uint64) private _stageSteps;
    /// Per target: the latest eta + GRACE_PERIOD of a counted stage step.
    mapping(address => uint64) private _stageUntil;
    /// The operation that last named each parameter key; a key is pending while that one is Queued (SWAPGOV-04).
    mapping(bytes32 => bytes32) private _keyOp;

    constructor(address proposer_, address guardian_) {
        if (proposer_ == address(0)) revert ZeroAddress();
        // GV-17: the canceller is never the scheduler.
        if (proposer_ == guardian_) revert ProposerIsGuardian(proposer_);
        proposer = proposer_;
        guardian = guardian_;
    }

    function schedule(address target, bytes calldata payload) external returns (bytes32 opId, uint64 eta) {
        return _schedule(target, 0, payload);
    }

    function scheduleWithValue(address target, uint256 value, bytes calldata payload)
        external
        returns (bytes32 opId, uint64 eta)
    {
        return _schedule(target, value, payload);
    }

    function _schedule(address target, uint256 value, bytes calldata payload)
        private
        returns (bytes32 opId, uint64 eta)
    {
        if (msg.sender != proposer) revert NotProposer(msg.sender);
        if (target == address(0)) revert ZeroAddress();
        (uint64 delay, bool valueTransfer) = _delayOf(payload);
        eta = uint64(block.timestamp) + delay;
        opId = keccak256(abi.encode(target, value, keccak256(payload), ++_nonce));

        Op storage op = _ops[opId];
        op.target = target;
        op.eta = eta;
        op.state = uint8(OpState.Queued);
        op.value = value;
        op.payload = payload;
        if (valueTransfer && !handedOver) {
            op.objectable = true;
            op.delay = delay;
            op.queueEpoch = uint64(ArkEpoch.current());
        }
        bytes4 selector = bytes4(payload[:4]);
        if (selector == ArkGovTables.SET_PARAMS_DIFF) _lockKeys(opId, payload);
        if (selector == ArkGovTables.SET_THREAT_STAGE) {
            // One Queued stage step per target (OC-GOV-02): steps run in sequence, each its own delay.
            if (_stageQueued(target)) revert StagePending(target);
            op.stageStep = true;
            // Counted for its target alone (re-audit 2026-09-30, SWAPGOV-09).
            // None is Queued (the refusal above): any count left is of steps
            // that expired unexecuted, so the count starts afresh at one.
            _stageSteps[target] = 1;
            _noteStageUntil(target, eta);
        } else if (
            selector == ArkGovTables.SET_PHASE || selector == ArkGovTables.ENTER_QDAY || selector == ArkGovTables.RESTORE
        ) {
            // One ecosystem phase count for every reader (2026-10-07). The
            // defensive enterQDay is never held behind a queued phase step.
            bool queued = _stageQueued(PHASE_STEPS);
            if (queued && selector != ArkGovTables.ENTER_QDAY) revert StagePending(target);
            op.stageStep = true;
            op.phaseStep = true;
            if (queued) {
                _stageSteps[PHASE_STEPS] += 1;
                _noteStageUntil(PHASE_STEPS, eta);
            } else {
                // Nothing counted is Queued: restart both, so the latest time of a
                // cancelled 30-day step does not outlive a later 48-hour one.
                _stageSteps[PHASE_STEPS] = 1;
                _stageUntil[PHASE_STEPS] = eta + GRACE_PERIOD;
            }
        }
        emit Scheduled(opId, target, selector, value, eta, delay, payload);
    }

    /**
     * One queued operation per parameter key (re-audit 2026-09-30,
     * SWAPGOV-04; GV-01): a keyed diff naming a key that an operation still
     * Queued also names is refused (`KeyPending`), so concurrent operations
     * can no longer chain their quarter-steps — four of them used to walk a
     * key across its whole range after one delay, in one block. An operation
     * executed, cancelled or expired frees its keys. The payload was decoded
     * and checked by `_delayOf`.
     */
    function _lockKeys(bytes32 opId, bytes calldata payload) private {
        IArkStakeGovernable.ParamChange[] memory changes =
            abi.decode(payload[4:], (IArkStakeGovernable.ParamChange[]));
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < changes.length; i++) {
            bytes32 k = changes[i].key;
            bytes32 prev = _keyOp[k];
            if (prev != bytes32(0) && stateOf(prev) == OpState.Queued) revert KeyPending(k, prev);
            _keyOp[k] = opId;
        }
    }

    function execute(bytes32 opId) external payable returns (bytes memory result) {
        Op storage op = _ops[opId];
        OpState s = stateOf(opId);
        if (s != OpState.Queued) revert OpNotQueued(opId, uint8(s));
        if (block.timestamp < op.eta) revert NotReady(op.eta, uint64(block.timestamp));
        if (msg.value != op.value) revert ValueMismatch(op.value, msg.value);

        op.state = uint8(OpState.Executed);
        if (op.stageStep) _uncountStageStep(_stageKey(op));
        bool ok;
        (ok, result) = op.target.call{value: msg.value}(op.payload);
        if (!ok) revert CallFailed(opId, result);
        emit Executed(opId, msg.sender);
    }

    function cancel(bytes32 opId) external {
        if (msg.sender != proposer && (msg.sender != guardian || guardian == address(0))) {
            revert NotProposerOrGuardian(msg.sender);
        }
        OpState s = stateOf(opId);
        if (s != OpState.Queued) revert OpNotQueued(opId, uint8(s));
        Op storage op = _ops[opId];
        if (msg.sender != proposer && op.target == address(this)) _guardianMayCancel(opId, op);
        op.state = uint8(OpState.Cancelled);
        if (op.stageStep) _uncountStageStep(_stageKey(op));
        emit Cancelled(opId, msg.sender);
    }

    /**
     * GV-03: the guardian cannot keep itself. It never cancels the S3
     * operation that removes it, and cancels a handover only once Leg B has
     * contested it (its S1 chamber role); an uncontested handover is the
     * proposer's to withdraw.
     */
    function _guardianMayCancel(bytes32 opId, Op storage op) private view {
        bytes memory p = op.payload;
        bytes4 s;
        if (p.length >= 4) s = bytes4(p[0]) | (bytes4(p[1]) >> 8) | (bytes4(p[2]) >> 16) | (bytes4(p[3]) >> 24);
        if (s == ArkGovTables.RENOUNCE_GUARDIAN || (s == ArkGovTables.HAND_OVER && !op.contested)) {
            revert GuardianCannotCancel(opId);
        }
    }

    /**
     * @inheritdoc IArkTimelock
     * @dev For the CALLER as the target (re-audit 2026-09-30, SWAPGOV-09): the
     *      staking parameter set asks for itself, so a stage payload aimed at
     *      any other account — one that can never move a stage there — holds
     *      nothing. `stageStepQueuedFor` reads any target.
     */
    function stageStepQueued() external view returns (bool) {
        return stageStepQueuedFor(msg.sender);
    }

    /// @inheritdoc IArkTimelock
    function stageStepQueuedFor(address target) public view returns (bool) {
        return _stageQueued(target) || _stageQueued(PHASE_STEPS);
    }

    /// @inheritdoc IArkTimelock
    function phaseStepQueued() external view returns (bool) {
        return _stageQueued(PHASE_STEPS);
    }

    function _stageQueued(address k) private view returns (bool) {
        return _stageSteps[k] != 0 && block.timestamp <= _stageUntil[k];
    }

    /// Where an operation's stage step is counted: its target, or the one phase count.
    function _stageKey(Op storage op) private view returns (address) {
        return op.phaseStep ? PHASE_STEPS : op.target;
    }

    function _uncountStageStep(address target) private {
        uint64 steps = _stageSteps[target];
        // An expired step can neither execute nor be cancelled, so a count
        // restarted after one never goes below zero; the guard is defensive.
        if (steps != 0) _stageSteps[target] = steps - 1;
    }

    function _noteStageUntil(address target, uint64 eta) private {
        uint64 until = eta + GRACE_PERIOD;
        if (until > _stageUntil[target]) _stageUntil[target] = until;
    }

    /**
     * Names the governor `handOver` hands the proposer role to, once, by the
     * proposer (S1). A false name gains nothing: the handover to it is still
     * a 30-day operation Leg B can contest and the guardian can cancel.
     */
    function setGovernor(address governor_) external {
        if (msg.sender != proposer) revert NotProposer(msg.sender);
        if (governor != address(0)) revert AlreadySet(governor);
        if (governor_ == address(0) || governor_ == address(this)) revert ZeroAddress();
        governor = governor_;
        emit GovernorSet(governor_);
    }

    /**
     * Hands the proposer role to the governor. One-way (G15): the previous
     * proposer keeps nothing, and no function restores it.
     *
     * @dev Only as a 30-day self-operation, and only to `governor` (re-audit
     *      2026-09-30, SWAPGOV-03): scheduled at S1 it is objectable by Leg B
     *      like any value-transfer operation, since the handover ends the S1
     *      chamber. Called directly, the S1 multisig handed the role to any
     *      key it held and closed the chamber for good, with no governor, no
     *      vote and no delay (GV-13, GV-17).
     */
    function handOver(address newProposer) external {
        if (msg.sender != address(this)) revert NotProposer(msg.sender);
        if (newProposer == address(0) || newProposer != governor) revert NotGovernor(newProposer);
        if (newProposer == proposer) revert SameProposer(newProposer);
        emit ProposerHandedOver(proposer, newProposer);
        proposer = newProposer;
        handedOver = true;
    }

    /**
     * Wires Leg B's principal, once. The S1 proposer may call it directly, so the
     * chamber is open from the start of S1 rather than 30 days into it; the
     * timelock itself may through a 30-day self-operation (the governor never
     * calls it). A captured proposer that points it at a false source gains
     * nothing it would not gain by never wiring it, and does so in public.
     */
    function setObjectionSource(address source) external {
        if (msg.sender != proposer && msg.sender != address(this)) revert NotProposer(msg.sender);
        if (objectionSource != address(0)) revert AlreadySet(objectionSource);
        if (source == address(0)) revert ZeroAddress();
        objectionSource = source;
        emit ObjectionSourceSet(source);
    }

    /**
     * A Leg B holder objects to a Queued S1 value-transfer operation, once, with
     * the principal it held at the boundary that opened the queue epoch: principal
     * subscribed after the operation became visible cannot object, and a transfer
     * during the window cannot count the same principal twice. At the threshold
     * the operation is contested and its eta moves to now + its delay.
     */
    function object(bytes32 opId) external {
        address src = objectionSource;
        if (src == address(0)) revert ObjectionSourceUnset();
        Op storage op = _ops[opId];
        if (!op.objectable || op.contested) revert OpNotObjectable(opId);
        OpState s = stateOf(opId);
        if (s != OpState.Queued) revert OpNotQueued(opId, uint8(s));
        if (_objected[opId][msg.sender]) revert OpAlreadyObjected(opId, msg.sender);

        uint256 w = IObjectionSource(src).principalAt(msg.sender, op.queueEpoch);
        if (w == 0) revert NoPrincipal(msg.sender);
        _objected[opId][msg.sender] = true;
        uint256 objection = op.objection + w;
        op.objection = objection;
        emit OpObjected(opId, msg.sender, w, objection);

        uint256 total = IObjectionSource(src).totalPrincipalAt(op.queueEpoch);
        if (objection * BPS >= uint256(OBJECTION_THRESHOLD_BPS) * total) {
            op.contested = true;
            uint64 eta = uint64(block.timestamp) + op.delay;
            op.eta = eta;
            if (op.stageStep) _noteStageUntil(_stageKey(op), eta);
            emit OpContested(opId, eta);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Removes the guardian for good (S3, design §7). No setter re-creates it.
     * Only as the timelock's own 30-day operation, the RenounceAdmin vote's:
     * the guardian used to renounce itself directly, ending the S1 and S2
     * cancel power without the S3 condition (GV-03).
     */
    function renounceGuardian() external {
        if (msg.sender != address(this)) revert NotGuardian(msg.sender);
        emit GuardianRenounced(guardian);
        guardian = address(0);
    }

    /**
     * Has `target` hand its governance to this timelock now (OC-GOV-03):
     * calls `target.acceptGovernance()`, which succeeds only where the
     * target's own governance proposed this contract. Anyone may: accepting
     * moves the target under every delay of the table and away from whoever
     * governed it, so it is not itself delayed. The deployer of the staking
     * leg proposes and has it accepted in the same run, leaving no window in
     * which its key governs and no `pendingGovernance` behind.
     */
    function acceptGovernanceOf(address target) external {
        IGovernanceAccept(target).acceptGovernance();
        emit GovernanceAccepted(target, msg.sender);
    }

    function delayFor(bytes calldata payload) external pure returns (uint64 delay) {
        (delay,) = _delayOf(payload);
    }

    /// The delay and whether the payload is one the Leg B chamber watches (OC-GOV-01): the governor's one source.
    function ruleFor(bytes calldata payload) external pure returns (uint64 delay, bool valueTransfer) {
        return _delayOf(payload);
    }

    function objectionOf(bytes32 opId)
        external
        view
        returns (bool objectable, bool contested, uint64 queueEpoch, uint256 objection)
    {
        Op storage op = _ops[opId];
        return (op.objectable, op.contested, op.queueEpoch, op.objection);
    }

    function keyRule(bytes32 key) external pure returns (uint64 delay, uint256 step) {
        return ArkGovTables.ruleOfKey(key);
    }

    function stateOf(bytes32 opId) public view returns (OpState) {
        Op storage op = _ops[opId];
        OpState s = OpState(op.state);
        if (s == OpState.Queued && block.timestamp > uint256(op.eta) + GRACE_PERIOD) return OpState.Expired;
        return s;
    }

    function etaOf(bytes32 opId) external view returns (uint64) {
        return _ops[opId].eta;
    }

    function opOf(bytes32 opId) external view returns (address target, uint256 value, uint64 eta, bytes memory payload) {
        Op storage op = _ops[opId];
        return (op.target, op.value, op.eta, op.payload);
    }

    /**
     * The table lookup, plus the two pace rules a payload can be checked for here.
     *
     * For a keyed diff: every key must be known, none may repeat (a repeat would
     * let one operation walk a key twice its step), and a value-transfer key may
     * move at most its step from the value the proposer declared as `expected`.
     * `expected` is the target's compare-and-swap operand, so a step measured
     * against it is a step measured against the value actually replaced.
     *
     * For a stage move: exactly one step, which the target checks as well.
     * For an ecosystem phase move: `setPhase` one step within 0..2 and
     * `enterQDay` from 0..2 (ArkPhase checks both against the phase in force).
     *
     * `valueTransfer` is true for a stage move, for the three phase moves, for a diff that names at
     * least one key with a step, and for `handOver`, `proposeGovernance`,
     * `setObjectionSource` and `setCourt` (OC-GOV-01) and the valuation
     * oracle's `pause()` renewal: the operations the Leg B chamber watches. A
     * step flagged `RELATIVE` (the oracle's `arkReferencePrice`) bounds the move
     * to that many bps of `expected`.
     */
    function _delayOf(bytes calldata payload) private pure returns (uint64 delay, bool valueTransfer) {
        if (payload.length < 4) revert PayloadTooShort(payload.length);
        bytes4 selector = bytes4(payload[:4]);
        delay = ArkGovTables.delayOfSelector(selector);
        if (delay == 0) revert UnknownSelector(selector);

        if (selector == ArkGovTables.SET_THREAT_STAGE) {
            (uint8 fromStage, uint8 toStage) = abi.decode(payload[4:], (uint8, uint8));
            uint256 f = fromStage;
            uint256 t = toStage;
            if (t != f + 1 && t + 1 != f) revert StageStepInvalid(fromStage, toStage);
            valueTransfer = true;
        } else if (selector == ArkGovTables.SET_PHASE) {
            (uint8 fromPhase, uint8 toPhase) = abi.decode(payload[4:], (uint8, uint8));
            uint256 f = fromPhase;
            uint256 t = toPhase;
            if (f > 2 || t > 2 || (t != f + 1 && t + 1 != f)) revert StageStepInvalid(fromPhase, toPhase);
            valueTransfer = true;
        } else if (selector == ArkGovTables.ENTER_QDAY) {
            uint8 fromPhase = abi.decode(payload[4:], (uint8));
            if (fromPhase > 2) revert StageStepInvalid(fromPhase, 3);
            valueTransfer = true;
        } else if (selector == ArkGovTables.RESTORE) {
            valueTransfer = true;
        } else if (
            selector == ArkGovTables.HAND_OVER || selector == ArkGovTables.PROPOSE_GOVERNANCE
                || selector == ArkGovTables.SET_OBJECTION_SOURCE || selector == ArkGovTables.SET_COURT
                || selector == ArkGovTables.PAUSE
        ) {
            // The handover ends the S1 chamber: objectable like a value transfer (SWAPGOV-03).
            // So does what hands a governed contract to another governance, or sets a
            // chamber's source or the governor's court once (OC-GOV-01). A pause renewal
            // prolongs the oracle's silence past what the guardian may impose alone.
            valueTransfer = true;
        } else if (delay == ArkGovTables.KEYED) {
            IArkStakeGovernable.ParamChange[] memory changes =
                abi.decode(payload[4:], (IArkStakeGovernable.ParamChange[]));
            uint256 n = changes.length;
            if (n == 0) revert EmptyDiff();
            if (n > MAX_DIFF) revert DiffTooLarge(n, MAX_DIFF);
            delay = 0;
            for (uint256 i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                IArkStakeGovernable.ParamChange memory c = changes[i];
                (uint64 d, uint256 step) = ArkGovTables.ruleOfKey(c.key);
                if (d == 0) revert UnknownKey(c.key);
                for (uint256 j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                    if (changes[j].key == c.key) revert DuplicateKey(c.key);
                }
                if (step != 0) {
                    uint256 moved = c.value > c.expected ? c.value - c.expected : c.expected - c.value;
                    // A relative step (arkReferencePrice): bps of the value replaced.
                    bool over = (step & ArkGovTables.RELATIVE) != 0
                        ? moved * BPS > c.expected * (step ^ ArkGovTables.RELATIVE)
                        : moved > step;
                    if (over) revert StepTooLarge(c.key, c.expected, c.value, step);
                    valueTransfer = true;
                }
                if (d > delay) delay = d;
            }
        }
    }
}
